; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Sacks Spiral
; CATEGORY: Number Theory
; LEVEL:    Advanced
; TAGS:     primes, polar coordinates, square roots, spiral, sieve
; SUMMARY:  Places every number on a smooth spiral where the perfect squares line up due east, and lights up the primes to show their curved streams.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   In 1994 Robert Sacks tried a variation on Ulam's square spiral.
;   Number n goes at distance sqrt(n) from the center, turned through
;   sqrt(n) full turns. That means 1, 4, 9, 16, 25 ... (the perfect
;   squares) all land on the line pointing right, and each turn of the
;   spiral holds one more number than the turn before. When the primes
;   are lit up, they gather on curves that sweep out from the center.
;   Numbers on one curve share a formula: one famous curve is
;   n*n + n + 41, which Euler noticed gives primes for n = 0 to 39.
;   Those numbers are drawn in orange.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run and watch the spiral fill outward. Change :TOTAL for more
;   or fewer numbers; :SCALE controls the size.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * polar coordinates: a distance and an angle instead of x and y
;   * turning an angle into x and y with SIN and COS
;   * a sieve array for fast prime tests
;   * property lists (PPROP and GPROP) as a quick "is this in the set?" table
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Mark the numbers n*n + n (the pronic numbers) in another color.
;     Where do they go?
;   * Draw only the composites instead. Do the curves show as gaps?
; ============================================================

to make.sieve :limit
  localmake "s (array :limit 1)
  for [i 1 :limit] [setitem :i :s "true]
  setitem 1 :s "false
  for [p 2 sqrt :limit] [
    if item :p :s [for (list "m :p * :p :limit :p) [setitem :m :s "false]]
  ]
  output :s
end

; Remember Euler's numbers n*n + n + 41 in a property list called
; "euler, so testing one is just a GPROP.
to mark.euler
  for [n 0 200] [
    localmake "e :n * :n + :n + 41
    if :e <= :total [pprop "euler :e "true]
  ]
end

to eulerp :n
  output not emptyp gprop "euler :n
end

; Polar to screen: radius :scale * sqrt n, angle sqrt n whole turns.
; The angle is measured counterclockwise from due east, the way
; mathematicians usually do it.
to place :n
  localmake "r :scale * sqrt :n
  localmake "a 360 * sqrt :n
  output (list :r * cos :a  :r * sin :a)
end

to spiral
  for [n 2 :total] [
    if item :n :primes [
      ifelse eulerp :n [setpencolor [255 150 40] setpensize 4] ~
                       [setpencolor [120 190 255] setpensize 2.5]
      dot place :n
    ]
    ; pause now and then so you can watch it grow
    if (remainder :n 400) = 0 [wait 1]
  ]
end

; A faint line through the perfect squares, which all sit due east.
to squares.line
  setpencolor [70 70 100] setpensize 1
  penup setxy 0 0 pendown setxy :scale * sqrt :total 0 penup
end

make "total 20000
make "scale 1.85
make "primes make.sieve :total

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [12 14 30]
mark.euler
squares.line
spiral
penup setxy -390 276 setpencolor [220 220 240] setlabelheight 16
label [Sacks spiral: primes in blue, n*n+n+41 in orange]
